# CA1031: five catches on the best-effort wake transport, one reason each. A wake is always published
# after its durable mutation committed, so a failed publish must never fail the caller - escaping would
# report failure for work that already succeeded - and StackExchange.Redis surfaces connection, timeout,
# command and disposal faults as unrelated types, so only a general catch covers "Redis is unreachable".
# The lazy subscribe runs inside a claim loop's first wait, so escaping would fault that loop and take
# the worker down because Redis was momentarily unreachable. The jittered relay runs detached off a
# callback thread and is never awaited, so escaping would become an unobserved Task exception, and its
# catch also clears the pending-jitter slot that channel needs to schedule again. Both dispose shapes
# unsubscribe during container teardown, where escaping would abort the disposal of every singleton
# after this one. Waiters keep their poll floors throughout.
[src/Acta.Redis/Features/Workers/RedisWakeup.cs]
dotnet_diagnostic.CA1031.severity = none

# CA1031: two catches that tear down a failed attempt and must never become the failure themselves. A
# rollback on a connection the database already aborted is the likeliest thing here to throw, and
# escaping would replace the attempt's own exception - DeadlockRetry classifies whatever leaves this
# scope, so a dispose error in a transient's place would abandon a retry that would have succeeded.
# Silent toward the caller, which rethrows the original failure; each swallowed teardown failure is
# logged at warning so an operator still sees a connection that never returned to the pool.
[src/Acta.Relational/Connections/DbSession.cs]
dotnet_diagnostic.CA1031.severity = none

# CA2208: RaiseJobAlertAsync's 'jobId' is not a parameter of that method by accident - it is the
# unknown-job signal itself. AlertsJob classifies the failure with when (ex.ParamName == "jobId") and
# the conformance spec pins the shape, so the paramName is a protocol token between the provider and
# the projection, not a misnamed argument. Renaming it to 'command' would break that filter and let a
# null-command ArgumentNullException masquerade as an unknown job.
[src/Acta.Relational/Stores/RelationalAlertStore.cs]
dotnet_diagnostic.CA2208.severity = none

# CA1031: spawning an OS clipboard tool fails in as many shapes as there are platforms - a missing
# binary, a denied spawn, an unsupported platform, a session with no clipboard at all - and none of them
# is worth crashing a CLI verb over. Returning null is the signal rather than silence: the caller
# reports the usage error that the value was neither passed nor readable from the clipboard.
[src/Acta.Runtime/Cli/CliClipboard.cs]
dotnet_diagnostic.CA1031.severity = none

# CA1031: top-level CLI handler. Any command failure has to become one line on stderr and exit code 1,
# not an unhandled exception the host prints as a stack trace, and the stream flush plus
# Environment.Exit below it must still run. Cancellation keeps its own arm above (exit 130).
[src/Acta.Runtime/Cli/CliCommandHost.cs]
dotnet_diagnostic.CA1031.severity = none

# CA1031: alert transports are user-supplied and may throw anything. A throwing transport must degrade
# to Retryable for that one channel; escaping would fail the whole sys.alerts job and stall delivery for
# every other alert and channel in the tick. The caller's own cancellation is rethrown by the filtered
# arm above, so shutdown is never recorded as a transport fault.
[src/Acta.Runtime/Modules/Alerting/AlertsJob.cs]
dotnet_diagnostic.CA1031.severity = none

# CA1031: three catches on the buffered-completion flush, none of them silent. The batch call logs at
# error and leaves every job Executing for sys.recovery - one statement, one commit, so nothing landed.
# The per-job catch records the first failure and the unfinalized job ids, reported together after the
# loop; the wakeup catch reports after the loop too, and a lost wakeup only costs a waiter its poll
# interval. This runs on the sink's own drain task, so escaping would kill the flush loop and strand
# every batch behind it.
[src/Acta.Runtime/Modules/Execution/CompletionSink.cs]
dotnet_diagnostic.CA1031.severity = none

# CA1031: per-tick catch on a long-lived background loop. A failed reload tick - a database outage, a
# row a provider rejects - must cost one interval, not the loop: escaping would end policy reloads for
# the process lifetime with no way to restart them, leaving every worker on stale definition policy.
# Logged at error; shutdown leaves through the filtered cancellation arms.
[src/Acta.Runtime/Modules/Execution/Definitions/DefinitionPolicyReloader.cs]
dotnet_diagnostic.CA1031.severity = none

# CA1031: the executor's per-attempt boundary, where an arbitrary user handler's exception becomes a
# recorded Failed outcome with JobUnhandledException (or the non-retryable reason for NotImplemented /
# NotSupported), the job landed, the retry budget applied and the timeline written. Handlers can throw
# anything, so the catch cannot be narrowed. Escaping would tear down the worker loop and leave the row
# Executing until its lease expired - a durable job silently turned into a recovery case.
[src/Acta.Runtime/Modules/Execution/JobExecution.cs]
dotnet_diagnostic.CA1031.severity = none

# CA1031: best-effort release of the exclusive-key lease, on a path that runs after the attempt's
# outcome is already decided. Escaping would replace that outcome with a cleanup failure, reporting a
# job that ran correctly as failed. Recorded through RecordLockReleaseFailure; the lease TTL reclaims
# the key.
[src/Acta.Runtime/Modules/Execution/RuntimeJobContext.cs]
dotnet_diagnostic.CA1031.severity = none

# CA1001: the one disposable field is _tickGate, a SemaphoreSlim used purely as an async mutex around
# TickAsync. A SemaphoreSlim only holds an OS wait handle once AvailableWaitHandle is read and nothing
# in Acta reads it, so Dispose() would release nothing; the gate is entered by the lock-lease loop and
# by WorkerRuntime.RunHeartbeatOnceAsync, so a disposed gate would turn a harmless late tick into
# ObjectDisposedException. Created once per WorkerRuntime and alive as long as the process, so
# deliberately not disposable. The per-extend linked source inside TickAsync is separately
# 'using'-scoped.
# CA1031: two catches on the lock-lease loop - the immediate first tick and each periodic tick. An
# extend failure must cost one interval: escaping would end the loop, so every lock held by a running
# attempt would stop being extended and would expire mid-flight while the attempt kept running.
[src/Acta.Runtime/Modules/Execution/Workers/LockLeaseHeartbeat.cs]
dotnet_diagnostic.CA1001.severity = none
dotnet_diagnostic.CA1031.severity = none

# CA1001: the one disposable field is _tickGate, on the same reasoning as LockLeaseHeartbeat's - a
# SemaphoreSlim whose Dispose() would release nothing, entered here by the heartbeat loop, by the host's
# StampDrainingAsync during the drain, and by WorkerRuntime.RunHeartbeatOnceAsync, so disposing it would
# turn a harmless late tick into ObjectDisposedException. Deliberately not disposable.
# CA1031: two catches on the heartbeat loop - the immediate first tick and each periodic tick. Escaping
# would end the loop, so last_seen would stop advancing and lease recovery would reclaim this worker's
# jobs while it was still running them. A failed tick must cost one interval and nothing more.
[src/Acta.Runtime/Modules/Execution/Workers/WorkerHeartbeat.cs]
dotnet_diagnostic.CA1001.severity = none
dotnet_diagnostic.CA1031.severity = none

# CA1001: the one disposable field is _drainCts, and disposing it would reclaim nothing. It is a bare
# CancellationTokenSource - no CancelAfter, so no Timer is ever armed, and nothing in Acta reads
# Token.WaitHandle, so the lazily-created ManualResetEvent is never materialized. Its only registration
# is the linked source WorkerLoop builds in RunLoopAsync, which that method scopes with 'using'. Against
# zero benefit, IDisposable costs real safety: the source must stay usable for the whole process because
# BeginDrain arrives from the host's StopAsync, and the public constructor is a documented direct test
# seam whose callers would all have to take on a lifecycle they do not otherwise need.
[src/Acta.Runtime/Modules/Execution/Workers/WorkerRuntime.cs]
dotnet_diagnostic.CA1001.severity = none

# CA1031: four catches, one per loop. The claim loop is the process's entire supply of work and must
# survive a database outage - escaping would end claiming for the process lifetime and the worker would
# idle silently until restarted - so it logs at error and backs off a full safety interval, because the
# anti-spin floor is for deadline races, not error retry. The executor loop's per-job catch keeps one
# bad job from tearing that executor down and silently shrinking concurrency until a restart. The
# Direct-profile coordinator's claim-side catch must release the slots it acquired, or the permits taken
# for the failed claim would never be returned and the batch would shrink permanently. RunOneAsync is
# not awaited, so escaping there would only produce an unobserved Task exception, leaving no record of
# why the job died. Shutdown leaves through the filtered cancellation arms.
# CA2000: the 'slots' SemaphoreSlim is the coordinator's permit counter, shared with every
# fire-and-forget RunOneAsync task, each of which Releases it in a finally. The loop's own finally
# drains by re-acquiring all executorCount permits, so the coordinator resumes while the last Release is
# still unwinding inside the semaphore; disposing there would race it, and Release starts with a
# disposed check, so a clean drain could end in ObjectDisposedException in the claim/dispatch loop.
# Disposal would also reclaim nothing: the only disposable state a SemaphoreSlim holds is the wait
# handle behind AvailableWaitHandle, which no code in Acta reads. Left to the GC on purpose.
[src/Acta.Runtime/Modules/Execution/Workers/WorkerLoop.cs]
dotnet_diagnostic.CA1031.severity = none
dotnet_diagnostic.CA2000.severity = none

# CA1031: shutdown must run to completion. A worker loop that faulted during drain is surfaced by
# base.StopAsync; escaping here would abandon the remaining shutdown steps of every other runtime.
# Logged at warning; the budget-elapsed case has its own arm above.
[src/Acta.Runtime/Modules/Execution/Workers/WorkerRuntimeHost.cs]
dotnet_diagnostic.CA1031.severity = none

# CA1031: best-effort shutdown phase. The outer catch routes a per-item failure to the caller's
# onFailure reporter instead of faulting the Task.WhenAll, which would abandon the other items' stamps
# mid-shutdown. The inner bare catch is deliberately broad and deliberately silent: it guards the
# reporter itself, because logging cannot be allowed to fault a best-effort shutdown phase.
[src/Acta.Runtime/Modules/Execution/Workers/WorkerShutdownPhase.cs]
dotnet_diagnostic.CA1031.severity = none

